 "thread": "155igplj1tk",
 "subject": "Odd discrepancy bwetween lm() and linear regression using Stan on the radon example data set",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2015-01-01T00:31:38Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "4UvzbCMhCMoJ",
   "from": "Anthony Staines",
   "author": "634b6a9aa9ef0981",
   "date": "2014-12-31T20:53:03Z",
   "subject": "Odd discrepancy bwetween lm() and linear regression using Stan on the radon example data set",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<br><br>I&#39;m learning to use stan (rather slowly) and I&#39;ve found something odd.<br><br>Specifically, a linear regression, using the radon data, of log(radon) ~ log(uranium) gives radically different results when fitted with lm() and when fitted with rStan. The intercept is much too low, and the effect estimate is a little higher than the lm() estimate. By contrast, the linear regression of log(radon) against floor, gives almost exactly the same results each way. <br><br>The reason is, I think, because the draws for the intercept and the effect of uranium are highly negatively correlated (see the second pairs plot). This is turn relates, but I don&#39;t quite see how, to the fact that the uranium level is a property of the county (one of 85) where the house (one of 919) in which the measurement was done is located.<br><br>I attach a pdf with the details. Any thoughts (other than do graphs and simple analyses first!)?<br><br>Regards,<br>Anthony Staines<br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "0TWQWhNafl8J",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2014-12-31T22:32:17Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Odd discrepancy bwetween lm() and linear regression using Stan on the radon example data set",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "One thing you could do is try using Stan&#39;s optimize to see if it\r<br>finds the same mode.  The posterior mean isn&#39;t guaranteed to be near\r<br>the posterior mode (though I&#39;d expect them to be reasonably close for\r<br>a regression like this).  Maybe someone else will have an idea about\r<br>what&#39;s going on.\r<br>\r<br>For your model, you want to declare the sd parameter as\r<br>\r<br>  real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma;\r<br>\r<br>The constraint in the declaration should match the support in the model.\r<br>\r<br>Your Stan models didn&#39;t render in that knitr.  Here&#39;s what I did in knitr\r<br>to render Stan programs:\r<br>\r<br>```{r comment=NA}\r<br>file_path &lt;- &quot;soil_incubation.stan&quot;;\r<br>lines &lt;- readLines(file_path, encoding=&quot;ASCII&quot;);\r<br>for (n in 1:length(lines)) cat(lines[n],&#39;\\n&#39;);\r<br>```\r<br>\r<br>I couldn&#39;t find any built-in way to include a file in knitr,\r<br>but I&#39;d love to hear better suggestions than the above hack.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Dec 31, 2014, at 3:53 PM, Anthony Staines &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">anthony...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hi,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I&#39;m learning to use stan (rather slowly) and I&#39;ve found something odd.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
